{#- T2 — performance & security opportunities. One repo, with file bodies,
    findings, and dependency annotations. The model MUST separate confirmed
    issues from speculative ones (FR-9.1). Context: docs/prompt-context.md. -#}
{% set repo = repos[0] -%}
You are a senior engineer doing a performance and security pass over the
repository **{{ repo.name }}**. Work only from what is provided below. When you are
inferring rather than observing, say so.

Generated {{ generated_at }}. Advisory data was {{ advisory_freshness | freshness_phrase }}.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REPOSITORY PROFILE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Name: {{ repo.name }}
Category: {{ repo.category }}
{%- if repo.health %} · health {{ repo.health.score }}/100 ({{ repo.health.band }}){% endif %}
{% if repo.git and repo.git.branch %}
Branch: {{ repo.git.branch }}
{%- if repo.git.last_commit_at %} · last commit {{ repo.git.last_commit_at }}{% endif %}
{% endif %}

Languages:
{% for l in repo.languages -%}
  - {{ l.language }} — {{ l.percentage | round(1) }}% ({{ l.code_lines }} lines)
{% endfor -%}
{% if repo.technologies %}
Technologies:
{% for t in repo.technologies -%}
  - {{ t.tech }} ({{ t.kind }})
{% endfor -%}
{% endif %}

Dependencies: {{ repo.dependency_counts.direct_total }} direct, {{ repo.dependency_counts.transitive_total }} transitive.
Direct dependencies (a leading ! marks one with a matched advisory):
{% for d in repo.direct_dependencies -%}
  - {% if d.advisories %}! {% endif %}{{ d.ecosystem }} {{ d.name }}@{{ d.version }} ({{ d.scope }}, version {{ d.version_confidence }})
{%- if d.advisories %} — {{ d.advisories | join(", ") }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KNOWN SECURITY FINDINGS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{% if repo.findings -%}
These come from matching dependencies against an advisory database. CONFIRMED
matched an exact locked version; SPECULATIVE matched only a version range and may
not apply to the version that actually resolves.
{% for f in repo.findings %}
- [{{ "CONFIRMED" if f.confirmed else "SPECULATIVE" }}] {{ f.kind | upper }} {{ f.advisory_id }}
  {{ f.package }}@{{ f.version }} · severity {{ f.severity }}
  {%- if f.fixed_version %} · fixed in {{ f.fixed_version }}{% endif %}
  {{ f.summary }}
{% endfor -%}
{% else -%}
No advisory matches were recorded for this repository
{%- if advisory_freshness == "Never" %}, but the advisory database has never been
synced, so treat that as "unknown", not "clean"{% endif %}.
{% endif %}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SOURCE ({{ scope.kind }})
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{% if files -%}
{% for file in files %}
### {{ file.path }}{% if file.language %} ({{ file.language }}){% endif %}

```{{ file.language | lower if file.language else "" }}
{{ file.content }}
```
{% endfor -%}
{% else -%}
(No file bodies were included — reason from the profile and findings above.)
{% endif %}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT TO PRODUCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two clearly separated sections:

## Confirmed issues
Problems you can point at a specific line, dependency, or advisory. For each: the
location, what is wrong, the impact, and the fix. Include the known security
findings above that are marked CONFIRMED and still apply.

## Speculative opportunities
Improvements that depend on context you cannot see (runtime load, data sizes,
deployment shape) or on a SPECULATIVE finding. For each: what you would change,
what evidence would confirm it is worth doing, and the risk of doing it blindly.

Within each section, order by impact. Do not pad — if a section is short, it is
short. Call out anything in the source that looks deliberately security-relevant
(auth, crypto, input parsing, subprocess, deserialization) even if it is fine.
